== Stage 5 ==
;27 August 2025 – [[Figueres]] to Figueres, {{convert|20|km|abbr=on}} ([[Team time trial|TTT]])

The race reached Spain on the fifth stage as the riders tackled a {{convert|24.1|km|adj=on}} flat team time trial in [[Figueres]]. The teams set off at four-minute intervals in the reverse order of their team classification standing with the exception of {{UCI team code|GFC|2025|nolink=y}}, which was the last team off the start ramp by virtue of having the general classification leader, Gaudu. Each team’s time was set by their fourth rider across the line.

The first team off the start ramp, {{UCI team code|LOT|2025|nolink=y}}, set the initial benchmark time of 25’ 53”. Their team was not beaten until the seventh team off the start ramp, {{UCI team code|LTK men|2025|nolink=y}}, was able to do so. They set a time of 25’ 35”, becoming the first team to average more than 56 kilometres per hour. Their time was threatened by {{UCI team code|IGD|2025|nolink=y}}, which set a time that was 10 seconds faster by the first time check. However, they slowed towards the end, eventually finishing with a time of 25’ 42”, seven seconds slower than {{UCI team code|LTK men|2025|nolink=y}}. {{UCI team code|RBH|2025|nolink=y}} was in contention for the stage win and despite being more than ten seconds slower by the second time check, they managed to threaten {{UCI team code|LTK men|2025|nolink=y}}’s time before falling short  by just three seconds. Three more teams, {{UCI team code|UAD men|2025|nolink=y}}, {{UCI team code|TVL men|2025|nolink=y}}, and {{UCI team code|GFC|2025|nolink=y}}, were able to beat {{UCI team code|LTK men|2025|nolink=y}}’s time at the first time check before falling behind by the second time check. Eventually, the best performance was ridden by {{UCI team code|UAD men|2025|nolink=y}}. They took 14 seconds from {{UCI team code|LTK men|2025|nolink=y}} in the final part of the course to set a time of 25’ 26”, averaging almost 57 kilometres per hour. {{UCI team code|TVL men|2025|nolink=y}} eventually finished second on the day, going eight seconds slower than the winners. With Gaudu losing 16 seconds to Vingegaard on the day, the Dane regained the red jersey by eight seconds ahead of the {{UCI team code|UAD men|2025|nolink=y}} trio of [[Juan Ayuso]], [[João Almeida (cyclist)|João Almeida]], and Soler.<ref name="CN-5" /><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cyclingweekly.com/racing/uae-team-emirates-xrg-power-to-team-time-trial-win-on-stage-5-of-vuelta-a-espana-as-jonas-vingegaard-moves-back-into-red-jersey|title=UAE Team Emirates-XRG power to team time trial win on stage 5 of Vuelta a España, as Jonas Vingegaard moves back into red jersey|first=Adam|last=Becket|work=Cycling Weekly|date=27 August 2025|access-date=27 August 2025}}</ref>

Protestors with Palestinian flags and a banner reading "Neutrality is complicity. Boycott Israel"<ref>{{Cite web |title=Instagram |url=https://www.instagram.com/reel/DN3zbW4XprB/ |access-date=2025-08-28 |website=www.instagram.com}}</ref> blocked Israel-Premier Tech's team time trial.<ref>{{Cite web |author1=Adam Becket |date=2025-08-27 |title=Pro-Palestine protestors disrupt Israel-Premier Tech's team time trial on stage 5 at Vuelta a España |url=https://www.cyclingweekly.com/racing/pro-palestine-protestors-disrupt-israel-premier-techs-team-time-trial-on-stage-5-at-vuelta-a-espana |access-date=2025-08-27 |website=Cycling Weekly |language=en}}</ref> The team finished the stage in 19th place of 23, 54 seconds to the stage winners,<ref>{{Cite web |title=La Vuelta Ciclista a España 2025 Stage 5 (TTT) results |url=https://www.procyclingstats.com/race/vuelta-a-espana/2025/stage-5 |access-date=2025-08-27 |website=www.procyclingstats.com}}</ref> but were then awarded 15 seconds due to the protest, thus placing them in 14th place.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-08-27 |title=Israel - Premier Tech awarded 15-seconds improved time by jury after their TTT is hindered by protests |url=https://cyclinguptodate.com/cycling/israel-premier-tech-awarded-15-seconds-improved-time-by-jury-after-their-ttt-is-hindered-by-protests |access-date=2025-08-28 |website=CyclingUpToDate.com |language=en}}</ref> One protestor was arrested by the regional Catalan Police.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-08-28 |title=Vuelta a España: Pasa a disposición judicial un detenido por obstaculizar el paso del Israel-Premier Tech |url=https://www.esciclismo.com/actualidad/carretera/87662.html |access-date=2025-08-28 |website=EsCiclismo.com |language=spanish}}</ref> Vuelta's director, Javier Guillén, responded by stating "they cannot allow what happened" in Stage 5 with the Israel-Premier Tech team.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Labarga |first=Nacho |date=2025-08-27 |title=Javier Guillén y el parón por Palestina: "Vamos a presentar una denuncia, no podemos permitirlo" |url=https://www.marca.com/ciclismo/vuelta-espana/2025/08/27/javier-guillen-paron-palestina-presentar-denuncia-permitirlo.html |access-date=2025-08-28 |website=MARCA |language=es}}</ref> Some sports commentators have commended protestors, but disapproved that the protest affected only one team<ref>{{Cite web |last=verTele |first=Redacción |date=2025-08-28 |title=Carlos de Andrés denuncia la masacre en Gaza durante la protesta contra el equipo Israel en la Vuelta: "Genocidio tremendo" |url=https://www.eldiario.es/vertele/noticias/carlos-de-andres-denuncia-masacre-gaza-protesta-equipo-israel-la-vuelta-genocidio-tremendo_1_12561566.html |access-date=2025-08-28 |website=Vertele |language=es}}</ref> and for putting rider safety at risk during the protest.<ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nHlbLBJNQqU |title=VICIOUSLY Technical Team Time Trial |date=2025-08-27 |last=Chris Horner |access-date=2025-08-28 |via=YouTube}}</ref> Other commentators disapproved the protest all together affirming that the Vuelta is not the place for such protests.<ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UF0B-oW8p3k |title=⏱️ UAE puede con VISMA pero VINGEGAARD vuelve a ser LÍDER ♦️ |date=2025-08-27 |last=Javier Ares |access-date=2025-08-28 |via=YouTube}}</ref>
