== Stage 11 ==
;3 September 2025 – [[Bilbao]] to Bilbao, {{convert|157.4|km|abbr=on}}
The twelfth stage featured a hilly {{convert|167|km|adj=on}} stage that was expected to suit the puncheurs. There were seven categorized climbs that were scattered throughout the stage. In the last {{convert|57|km|abbr=}}, the riders tackled the second-category Alto del Vivero (4.2&nbsp;km at 8.3%) twice before heading towards the third-category Alto de Pike (2.3&nbsp;km at 8.9%), the final climb on the day. The last {{convert|1.2|km|abbr=}} of the Alto de Pike averaged over 12% with bonus seconds available at the top. From the top, there were {{convert|7.8|km|abbr=}} left to the finish in Bilbao.

The start of the stage was delayed due to a meeting between team representatives, CPA, and the race organizers with regards to safety issues from potential pro-Palestinian protests on the stage.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cyclingweekly.com/racing/we-are-just-cyclists-doing-our-job-vuelta-a-espana-riders-blame-pro-palestine-protests-for-causing-crash|title='We are just cyclists doing our job' – Vuelta a España riders blame pro-Palestine protests for causing crash, demonstration delays start of stage 11|first=Adam|last=Becket|work=Cycling Weekly|date=3 September 2025|access-date=3 September 2025}}</ref> With the uphill start to the stage, multiple riders attempted to get into the break. After just over {{convert|34|km|abbr=}} of racing, a group of three riders, Soler, Pedersen, and Aular, was able to get a significant gap on the peloton. Several riders tried to bridge to the trio but the front group was the only one allowed to get a gap. With {{convert|80|km|abbr=}} left, Soler soloed off the front while Pedersen and Aular were caught by the peloton. Soler himself was caught at the base of the first ascent of Alto del Vivero. On the climb, [[Mikel Landa]] ({{UCI team code|SOQ|2025|nolink=y}}) tried to form another break. He was soon joined by [[Santiago Buitrago]] ({{UCI team code|TBV|2025|nolink=y}}) while a group of six chasers, including Pedersen, went off in pursuit of them. The chasers were never able to get close to the front duo and were eventually swallowed up by the peloton. With {{convert|33|km|abbr=}} to go, Landa suffered from cramps, leaving Buitrago up front. Buitrago held a lead of close to 40 seconds heading to the second climb up Alto del Vivero.

On the climb, Almeida accelerated twice with his final attack bringing back Buitrago in the process. A group containing most of the {{abbr|GC|general classification}} contenders formed on the descent of the climb. On the approach to Alto de Pike, the race center announced that due to pro-Palestinian protests at the finish, the {{abbr|GC|general classification}} times were to be taken at the {{convert|3|km|adj=on}} mark and that there was going to be no stage winner. On the final climb, Pidcock attacked twice, distancing Vingegaard both times. At the top, Vingegaard gradually made his way back to Pidcock’s wheel while a group containing Almeida, Hindley, Gall, and Jorgenson formed behind the two. Pidcock and Vingegaard worked together to keep their gap over the chasers, crossing the {{convert|3|km|adj=on}} mark 10 seconds ahead of the four chasers. In the {{abbr|GC|general classification}}, Vingegaard extended his lead to 50 seconds on Almeida while Pidcock gained two bonus seconds on Vingegaard at the top of Alto de Pike, reducing his deficit to 56 seconds.<ref name="CN-11" /><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cyclingweekly.com/racing/jonas-vingegaard-and-tom-pidcock-take-time-on-rivals-on-stage-11-of-vuelta-a-espana-as-day-ends-without-a-winner|title=Jonas Vingegaard and Tom Pidcock take time on rivals on stage 11 of Vuelta a España as day ends without a winner|first=Adam|last=Becket|work=Cycling Weekly|date=3 September 2025|access-date=3 September 2025}}</ref>

Apart from the protests at the finish which caused the neutralization of the stage, there were more incidents on the stage stemming from the protests. Before the first passage of the finish line, several small white papers were thrown on the road while several protesters tried to invade the road. On the second time up Alto del Vivero, a protester with a banner blocked the road, causing riders to swerve to avoid the banner.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cyclingweekly.com/racing/more-pro-palestine-protests-affect-vuelta-a-espana-on-stage-11-in-bilbao|title=No winner on stage 11 of Vuelta a España due to pro-Palestine protests, general classification time taken with 3km to go|first=Adam|last=Becket|work=Cycling Weekly|date=3 September 2025|access-date=3 September 2025}}</ref>
